---
/**
 * Jev (TypeSafe) — hand-written model reference.
 *
 * Why this page is not generated from the snapshot: `typesafe/jev-1.13` is
 * served by OpenRouter but is absent from the `/api/v1/models` list that
 * `scripts/sync-openrouter-models.ts` reads, so nothing in the build would ever
 * create it. It is the one model page in the site that a human has to keep, and
 * the date stamp is the price of that.
 *
 * Contract carried over from the stealth-line pages: facts live in a FactTable
 * with a provenance line, other people's reporting lives in ClaimList with a
 * confidence tag, and the two never share a surface. The disambiguation block
 * exists because the bare word "jev" is not ours to claim.
 */
import System from "../../layouts/System.astro";
import JsonLd from "../../components/JsonLd.astro";
import FactTable from "../../components/FactTable.astro";
import ClaimList from "../../components/ClaimList.astro";
import TimelineList from "../../components/TimelineList.astro";
import Faq from "../../components/Faq.astro";
import SubscribeForm from "../../components/SubscribeForm.astro";
import { articleSchema, breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema, SITE, SITE_NAME } from "../../lib/schema";
import {
  JEV_CLOUDFLARE_URL,
  JEV_DATA_AS_OF,
  JEV_INPUT_PRICE_PER_MILLION,
  JEV_LISTED_AT_UTC,
  JEV_MODEL_ID,
  JEV_OPENROUTER_URL,
  JEV_TYPESAFE_BLOG_URL,
  alsoCalledJev,
  claims,
  jevFaqs,
  timeline,
  verifiedSpecs,
} from "@repo/lib/jev";
import {
  JEV_OURS_MEASURED_ON,
  JEV_OURS_SAMPLES,
  oursLabelStability,
  oursLatency,
  oursSampleResponse,
  oursSpread,
  oursUsage,
} from "@repo/lib/jev-our-measurements";

const title = "Jev on OpenRouter: TypeSafe's System One Model, Priced and Explained (2026)";
const h1 = "What is Jev? TypeSafe's decision model, on OpenRouter";
const description =
  "Jev is TypeSafe's System One model: text state in, typed decisions out, 32K context, $0.042 per million input tokens and free output. Listed on OpenRouter as typesafe/jev-1.13 — specs, price, and what it is not.";
const canonical = `${SITE}/typesafe-jev`;

const tldr = [
  ["Maker", "TypeSafe AI", "Announced 2026-09-15"],
  ["Class", "System One", "Decisions, not text"],
  ["OpenRouter ID", "typesafe/jev-1.13", "Listed 18 Sep 2026"],
  ["Context", "32,000", "tokens"],
  ["Input price", `$${JEV_INPUT_PRICE_PER_MILLION}`, "per million tokens"],
  ["Output price", "Free", "returns a decision"],
  ["Latency", "70–500 ms", "vendor-claimed"],
  ["Writes text?", "No", "gives up string generation"],
];

/** The three question types, in the shape the answer comes back in. */
const questionTypes = [
  {
    name: "Noul",
    shape: "a probability between 0 and 1",
    detail: "You name the true and false criteria; the model answers how likely the true one is.",
  },
  {
    name: "Choice",
    shape: "one option from a set you define",
    detail: "Routing, classification, triage — wherever the branches are already written down.",
  },
  {
    name: "Score",
    shape: "a position on an ordered scale you define",
    detail: "Grading, priors, severity. The scale is yours, so the output is already meaningful to code.",
  },
];

const vias = [
  {
    name: "OpenRouter",
    id: JEV_MODEL_ID,
    note: "One key, one bill, the same endpoint the rest of the directory is read from.",
    href: JEV_OPENROUTER_URL,
  },
  {
    name: "Cloudflare Workers AI",
    id: "typesafe/jev",
    note: "Listed as a third-party model; called through env.AI.run with a state and a questions object.",
    href: JEV_CLOUDFLARE_URL,
  },
];

const relatedPages = [
  {
    href: "/comparison",
    title: "Compare the models that do write text",
    body: "15 LLMs side by side on price, context and fit — the tools Jev is meant to be called instead of, not alongside.",
  },
  {
    href: "/openrouter-models",
    title: "The full model directory",
    body: "Every model this site tracks, with the price and window read from the public catalogue.",
  },
  {
    href: "/stealth-models",
    title: "The Alpha line",
    body: "The other way a new model shows up on OpenRouter: anonymous, free, and gone in days. Jev did not arrive that way, and the difference matters.",
  },
];
---
<System title={`${title} | ${SITE_NAME}`} description={description} canonical={canonical} ogType="article">
  <JsonLd
    slot="head"
    data={articleSchema({
      title: "Jev (TypeSafe): the System One model on OpenRouter",
      description:
        "TypeSafe's Jev returns typed decisions with calibrated probabilities instead of generating text. Specs, price and question types, read from the public listings on 2026-09-18.",
      publishedAt: JEV_DATA_AS_OF,
      updatedAt: JEV_DATA_AS_OF,
      image: `${SITE}/og-image.png`,
      url: canonical,
    })}
  />
  <JsonLd slot="head" data={faqSchema(jevFaqs)} />
  <JsonLd
    slot="head"
    data={breadcrumbSchema([
      { name: "Home", url: SITE },
      { name: "OpenRouter models", url: `${SITE}/openrouter-models` },
      { name: "Jev (TypeSafe)", url: canonical },
    ])}
  />

  <p class="kicker">Model reference · verified</p>
  <h1 class="display">{h1}</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Jev is TypeSafe's first model, announced on 15 September 2026 and listed on OpenRouter three days
    later. It does not write. You hand it program state and a typed question, and it hands back a decision
    with a probability attached — the thing a chat model is worst at and a program needs most. This page
    carries the specs, the price, the three question types, and a clear list of what it is not.
  </p>
  <div class="byline">
    <span>Reference</span>
    <span>Listed {JEV_LISTED_AT_UTC}</span>
    <span>Verified {JEV_DATA_AS_OF}</span>
    <span>
      <a href={JEV_OPENROUTER_URL} rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenRouter listing →</a>
    </span>
  </div>

  {/*
    The container. `/typesafe-jev` is the cover of the Jev topic, not a leaf: the
    field notes are a chapter of it, and a reader who lands here should be able to
    see that without scrolling to the bottom. Entries in a topic are linked from the
    topic — never from the nav, which is why adding a chapter never touches the
    site chrome. See ADR-0018.
  */}
  <nav
    aria-label="In this topic"
    style="margin: var(--space-6) 0 var(--space-8); padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-6); border: 1px solid var(--field-border); border-left: 2px solid var(--accent); border-radius: var(--radius); background: var(--bg-sunken);"
  >
    <div style="font-family: var(--mono); font-size: var(--text-xs); color: var(--text-faint); letter-spacing: 0.06em;">
      IN THIS TOPIC
    </div>
    <ul style="margin: var(--space-3) 0 0; padding-left: var(--space-6); max-width: var(--measure);">
      <li>
        <strong>Jev, the reference</strong> — this page: what it is, what it costs,
        the three question types, and our own raw response.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/jev-guide">How people actually use it</a> — eleven projects read
        first-hand, plus twenty calls we made ourselves.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/field-notes">All field notes</a> — every chapter of this kind,
        across every model we have looked at this closely.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="tldr">The short version</h2>
  <div class="stats">
    {tldr.map(([label, value, sub]) => (
      <div class="stat">
        <div class="label">{label}</div>
        <div class="value" style="font-size: var(--text-xl);">{value}</div>
        <div class="sub">{sub}</div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2>Verified specs</h2>
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); max-width: var(--measure); margin-bottom: var(--space-4);">
    Read on {JEV_DATA_AS_OF} from the OpenRouter endpoint, the Cloudflare Workers AI model card and
    TypeSafe's own announcement. Every row can be re-checked at the links below; the vendor's claims are
    labelled as the vendor's claims, including the ones we would like to be true.
  </p>
  <FactTable rows={verifiedSpecs} />
  <div class="provenance">
    <span>Source <code>openrouter.ai/api/v1/models/typesafe/jev-1.13/endpoints</code></span>
    <span>Verified <code>{JEV_DATA_AS_OF}</code></span>
    <span>Announcement <a href={JEV_TYPESAFE_BLOG_URL} rel="noopener noreferrer">typesafe.ai</a></span>
  </div>

  <h2>What it actually returns</h2>
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); max-width: var(--measure);">
    Every call is a state plus a set of named questions. There are three question types, and the shape of
    each answer is fixed in advance — which is what makes the output safe to hand straight to code
    instead of parsing it and hoping.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="data">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Type</th><th>Comes back as</th><th>Use it for</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {questionTypes.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td><strong>{row.name}</strong></td>
            <td>{row.shape}</td>
            <td style="color: var(--text-muted);">{row.detail}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="provenance">
    <span>Source <code>developers.cloudflare.com/ai/models/typesafe/jev</code></span>
    <span>Vendor framing <a href={JEV_TYPESAFE_BLOG_URL} rel="noopener noreferrer">the launch post</a></span>
  </div>

  {/*
    Figure 1 — the shape of a call. Drawn rather than described because the whole
    product is the difference between these two arrows: what goes in is state,
    what comes back is a typed value you can branch on without parsing it.
    Colours come from the theme tokens so the light/dark palettes both hold.
  */}
  <figure style="margin: var(--space-8) 0; padding: var(--space-6); border: 1px solid var(--field-border); border-radius: var(--radius); background: var(--bg-sunken);">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 760 210" width="100%" role="img" style="display: block; max-width: 100%; height: auto;">
      <title>One Jev call: program state and typed questions go in, typed decisions with probabilities come out.</title>
      <defs>
        <marker id="jev-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
          <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="var(--text-faint)" />
        </marker>
      </defs>

      <rect x="6" y="46" width="196" height="60" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--field-border)" />
      <text x="20" y="72" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="13" fill="var(--text-faint)">IN</text>
      <text x="20" y="93" font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="15" fill="var(--text)">Program state</text>

      <rect x="6" y="120" width="196" height="72" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--field-border)" />
      <text x="20" y="146" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="13" fill="var(--text-faint)">IN</text>
      <text x="20" y="167" font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="15" fill="var(--text)">Typed questions</text>
      <text x="20" y="184" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="12" fill="var(--text-muted)">Noul · Choice · Score</text>

      <path d="M 206 76 L 268 76" stroke="var(--text-faint)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#jev-arrow)" />
      <path d="M 206 156 L 268 156" stroke="var(--text-faint)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#jev-arrow)" />
      <path d="M 268 76 L 268 156" stroke="var(--text-faint)" stroke-width="1.5" />
      <path d="M 268 116 L 300 116" stroke="var(--text-faint)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#jev-arrow)" />

      <rect x="304" y="76" width="150" height="80" rx="8" fill="var(--accent)" />
      <text x="379" y="110" font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="18" font-weight="600" fill="var(--on-accent)" text-anchor="middle">Jev</text>
      <text x="379" y="132" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="12" fill="var(--on-accent)" text-anchor="middle">70–500 ms</text>

      <path d="M 458 116 L 494 116" stroke="var(--text-faint)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#jev-arrow)" />

      <rect x="498" y="86" width="256" height="60" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--field-border)" />
      <text x="512" y="112" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="13" fill="var(--text-faint)">OUT</text>
      <text x="512" y="133" font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="15" fill="var(--text)">A typed value — never malformed</text>

      <rect x="498" y="152" width="256" height="56" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--field-border)" />
      <text x="512" y="176" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="13" fill="var(--text-faint)">OUT</text>
      <text x="512" y="196" font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="15" fill="var(--text)">Confidence + probability</text>

      <text x="6" y="26" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="12" fill="var(--text-faint)">ONE CALL, NO PARSING STEP</text>
      <text x="754" y="26" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="12" fill="var(--text-faint)" text-anchor="end">NO STRING GENERATION</text>
    </svg>
    <figcaption style="margin-top: var(--space-4); font-size: var(--text-xs); color: var(--text-faint); max-width: var(--measure);">
      Figure 1 — why this is not a chat call. A chat model returns text that code has to parse and validate; Jev returns the decision, already typed, with the uncertainty attached. Diagram ours; the latency band is the vendor's claim.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  {/*
    Figure 2 — the vendor's two headline ranges on a logarithmic axis, because
    0.07 s and 329 s on a linear axis is one visible bar and one invisible dot.
    Log scale is stated on the figure so nobody reads the bar lengths as ratios.
  */}
  <figure style="margin: var(--space-8) 0; padding: var(--space-6); border: 1px solid var(--field-border); border-radius: var(--radius); background: var(--bg-sunken);">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 760 175" width="100%" role="img" style="display: block; max-width: 100%; height: auto;">
      <title>End-to-end latency on a logarithmic scale: Jev 70 to 500 milliseconds, frontier LLMs 3 to 329 seconds.</title>
      <text x="0" y="14" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="12" fill="var(--text-faint)">END-TO-END LATENCY · LOG SCALE · VENDOR'S FIGURES</text>

      {/*
        Row labels sit above their bars, not beside them: "Frontier LLM"
        anchored to the left edge of the plot ran off the viewBox and rendered
        as "ntier LLM" in production. The label has to live inside the width the
        axis already reserves.
      */}
      <line x1="60" y1="134" x2="700" y2="134" stroke="var(--field-border)" />
      {[
        [129, "0.1 s"],
        [267, "1 s"],
        [405, "10 s"],
        [543, "100 s"],
      ].map(([x, label]) => (
        <g>
          <line x1={x} y1="130" x2={x} y2="138" stroke="var(--field-border)" />
          <text x={x} y="154" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="11" fill="var(--text-faint)" text-anchor="middle">{label}</text>
        </g>
      ))}

      <text x="60" y="38" font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="14" fill="var(--text)">Jev</text>
      <rect x="108" y="46" width="118" height="18" rx="9" fill="var(--success)" />
      <text x="236" y="60" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="12" fill="var(--text-muted)">70–500 ms</text>

      <text x="60" y="94" font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="14" fill="var(--text)">Frontier LLM</text>
      <rect x="333" y="102" width="282" height="18" rx="9" fill="var(--text-faint)" opacity="0.45" />
      <text x="625" y="116" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="12" fill="var(--text-muted)">3–329 s</text>
    </svg>
    <figcaption style="margin-top: var(--space-4); font-size: var(--text-xs); color: var(--text-faint); max-width: var(--measure);">
      Figure 2 — the gap is real, and it is also the maker's own measurement, run from their machines on System One shaped queries. We have not reproduced it. Note the axis: at this scale the difference looks like roughly a decade, which is the same thing the vendor calls 40×–200×.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Why this model is being searched for</h2>
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); max-width: var(--measure);">
    The bare word “jev” sat flat for three months and then moved within a day of the announcement. The
    interesting part is not the size of the spike — it is which words come with it.
  </p>
  <TimelineList entries={timeline} />
  <div class="provenance">
    <span>Source <code>Google Trends, worldwide, trailing 3 months</code></span>
    <span>Read <code>{JEV_DATA_AS_OF}</code></span>
    <span>Index is relative to the term's own peak, not a search volume</span>
  </div>

  <h2>What the community claims</h2>
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); max-width: var(--measure); margin-bottom: var(--space-6);">
    None of this comes from TypeSafe, OpenRouter or Cloudflare. It is here because it is what people are
    actually saying, with the evidence attached so you can weigh it — and because the loudest claim of the
    week is the one nobody can check.
  </p>
  <ClaimList claims={claims} />

  <h2>How to call it</h2>
  <ol style="max-width: var(--measure); color: var(--text-muted); padding-left: var(--space-6);">
    <li>
      <strong style="color: var(--text);">Get access.</strong> Jev ships in early access, and TypeSafe's
      site runs a waitlist. OpenRouter and Cloudflare both list it already, so an account on either side is
      the shorter path.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong style="color: var(--text);">Pick a front door.</strong> Same model id on both; Cloudflare is
      the shorter hop if your code is already a Worker.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong style="color: var(--text);">Send state, not a prompt.</strong> The input is program state —
      the thing your code already has — plus questions with the criteria named in them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong style="color: var(--text);">Read the probability, not just the label.</strong> Every answer
      carries a confidence. Threshold it in your own code; that is the whole point of the design.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="data">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Where</th><th>Model id</th><th>Note</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {vias.map((via) => (
          <tr>
            <td><a href={via.href} rel="noopener noreferrer">{via.name}</a></td>
            <td><code>{via.id}</code></td>
            <td style="color: var(--text-muted);">{via.note}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>How it differs from the models in our directory</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="data">
      <thead>
        <tr><th></th><th>Chat LLM</th><th>Jev</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td style="color: var(--text-muted);">Sends back</td>
          <td>Text you parse and validate</td>
          <td><strong>A typed value, already valid</strong></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="color: var(--text-muted);">Says when it is unsure</td>
          <td>Only if prompted, and inconsistently</td>
          <td><strong>Every answer carries a probability</strong></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="color: var(--text-muted);">Output cost</td>
          <td>Roughly 5× the input price</td>
          <td><strong>Free</strong></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="color: var(--text-muted);">Latency</td>
          <td>Seconds — 3 to 329 s per the vendor</td>
          <td><strong>70–500 ms, vendor-claimed</strong></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="color: var(--text-muted);">Can it explain itself?</td>
          <td>Yes</td>
          <td>No — and that is the trade</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="provenance">
    <span>Comparison built from <a href={JEV_TYPESAFE_BLOG_URL} rel="noopener noreferrer">TypeSafe's own table</a></span>
    <span>Vendor claims are marked as such</span>
  </div>

  <h2>Four other things called Jev</h2>
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); max-width: var(--measure);">
    Three letters, four unrelated meanings, and all of them outrank this model on search volume. If you
    searched “jev” and wanted one of these, the table is the fastest way out — and if you wanted the model,
    it is the reason to add a qualifier.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="data">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Also “jev”</th><th>What it is</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {alsoCalledJev.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td style="white-space: nowrap;"><strong>{row.name}</strong></td>
            <td style="color: var(--text-muted);">{row.what}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  {/*
    The receipts section. Every other page on the web that covers Jev is
    summarising the same two sources; this table says which requests we actually
    made, when, and what came back — including the row where the answer was
    "we could not check this". That last row is the point: a verification log
    with no gaps in it is an advertisement, not a log.
  */}
  <h2>What we checked ourselves</h2>
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); max-width: var(--measure);">
    This page is not a summarised press release. Below is every read we made on
    {JEV_DATA_AS_OF}, what we asked, and what came back — including the one thing we
    could not check.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="data">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Question</th><th>How we asked</th><th>What came back</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Is Jev really on OpenRouter?</td>
          <td><code>GET /api/v1/models/typesafe/jev-1.13/endpoints</code></td>
          <td>200. Provider <strong>TypeSafe</strong>, modality <code>text-&gt;decisions</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>When was it listed, and where does that timestamp come from?</td>
          <td>the <code>created</code> field of the same response</td>
          <td><code>1789689684</code> — {JEV_LISTED_AT_UTC}. Read from the catalogue, not from the announcement.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>What does it actually cost?</td>
          <td>the <code>pricing</code> block of the same response</td>
          <td><code>prompt 0.000000042</code> per token (= ${JEV_INPUT_PRICE_PER_MILLION} per million), <code>completion 0</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Do the two catalogues agree on context?</td>
          <td>OpenRouter endpoint vs the Cloudflare model card</td>
          <td>Both say <strong>32,000</strong>. When two independent listings agree, we print the number without a hedge.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Why is this page hand-written?</td>
          <td>grep the whole <code>/api/v1/models</code> list (445 entries) for <code>jev</code> and <code>typesafe</code></td>
          <td><strong>No match.</strong> It is served, and absent from the list our sync reads. That is the finding this page exists to record.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>What are the question types, exactly?</td>
          <td>the code sample on the Cloudflare Workers AI model card</td>
          <td><code>noul</code> with named true/false criteria, <code>choice</code> with named options, <code>score</code> with an ordered list.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Is the speed and price claim real?</td>
          <td>the vendor's launch post, 2026-09-15</td>
          <td>They publish 70–500 ms, $0.042 per million in, output free. <strong>We have not reproduced any of it</strong> — so it is labelled as theirs everywhere on this page.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Is anyone actually searching for it?</td>
          <td>Google Trends, worldwide, three months, daily</td>
          <td>Index 1 for 92 of 93 days, then 4 → 26 → 61 → 100. Related queries are AI-side, not the rapper.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="background: var(--bg-sunken);">
          <td><strong>Does it work?</strong></td>
          <td>20 live calls, byte-identical, {JEV_OURS_MEASURED_ON}</td>
          <td>
            <strong>Yes</strong> — HTTP 200 on every call, model <code>jev-1.13.0</code>.
            The raw response and what 20 repeats did to it are in
            <a href="#our-run">Our own run</a> below.
          </td>
        </tr>
